<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck was a German general who commanded the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. With a small force of German and African askari troops he won the Battle of Tanga in 1914 and then waged a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign through Tanganyika, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>. He surrendered only on 25 November 1918, after the armistice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck.jpg" alt="Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photoatelier Hermann Noack, Berlin; cropped, retouched and resolution reduced by Jossi2 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Paul_von_Lettow-Vorbeck_LCCN2014719610b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paul Emil von Lettow-Vorbeck was born on 20 March 1870 into the Pomeranian minor nobility, the son of an army officer stationed at Saarlouis in the Prussian Rhine Province. He was educated in boarding schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and joined the cadet corps at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> and Berlin-Lichterfelde. In 1890 he was commissioned a Leutnant in the Imperial German Army and was later assigned to the Great German General Staff.</p><p>In 1900 he was posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with the international force against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a>. From 1904, as a captain, he served in German Southwest Africa during the Herero insurrection and the subsequent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, fighting at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterberg">Battle of Waterberg</a>. He also took part in the pursuit of the Nama leader Jacob Morenga, and was wounded in 1906, losing the sight of his left eye.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In August 1914 Lettow-Vorbeck commanded a garrison of 2,600 Germans and 2,472 African soldiers in fourteen askari field companies. He knew that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> would be a sideshow and meant to tie down as many British troops as possible, keeping them from the Western Front. Disregarding the colony's governor, Heinrich Schnee, who sought neutrality, he repelled a British amphibious assault on Tanga in the battle fought from 2 November 1914, and won a second victory at Jassin on 19 January 1915.</p><p>He then raided British East Africa, targeting forts, railways and communications, and expanded the Schutztruppe to some 14,000 soldiers, most of them askaris. For four years this force held in check a much larger one of British, Indian, Belgian and Portuguese troops. Lettow-Vorbeck returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in March 1919 to a hero's welcome. He left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichswehr">Reichswehr</a> in 1920 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a>, and sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> for the German National People's Party from 1928 to 1930.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under the Nazi regime Lettow-Vorbeck declined the post of head of the Reich Colonial Ministry and, in 1935, an ambassadorship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, though in 1939 Hitler gave him the honorary rank of General of the Infantry. His two sons were killed serving in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 1964, and two former askaris were flown in as guests to his funeral.</p><p>In the same year the West German Bundestag voted back pay for surviving askaris. Barracks of the Bundeswehr were named after him, and one, in Leer, remains. His memory is contested: in 2010 the council of Saarlouis renamed a street named for him, chiefly because of his part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a>. His book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, as I Saw It Again defends colonial rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the First World War General <b>Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck</b> led 3,500 Europeans and 12,000 Askari and porters against a British army of 40,000, at times under Jan Smuts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saarlouis, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Othmarschen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.32,6.75&amp;year=1931" aria-label="Saarlouis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4706 444 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5156 714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5156 714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.32,6.75&amp;year=1931">What was here in 1931? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
